<title>FRANCE: German minister warns French opposition on EMU.</title>
<headline>German minister warns French opposition on EMU.</headline>
<dateline>PARIS 1996-09-11</dateline>
<text>
<p>Germany's minister for Europe took the unusual step on Tuesday of publicly warning France's opposition Socialists against any weakening of the fight against inflation in the run-up to a single European currency.</p>
<p>Werner Hoyer, junior minister for European affairs, was responding in an article in the newspaper Le Monde to a call by Socialist parliamentary floor leader Laurent Fabius for a shift in Franco-German economic and monetary policy to revive growth.</p>
<p>&quot;On that point, Laurent Fabius is wrong on multiple counts,&quot; Hoyer wrote. &quot;His wish to use monetary policy as an instrument to revive the economy and to slacken the fight against inflation means nothing less than abandoning European economic and monetary union (EMU) before even joining it.&quot;</p>
<p>He also said Fabius was mistaken in wanting to enshrine the fight against unemployment in the European Union treaty as one of the aims of the 15-nation block.</p>
</text>
